## an8.14
an8.14:1.1 | S: “Mendicants, I will teach you about eight wild colts and eight defects in horses, and about eight wild people and eight defects in people. | T: “Monks, I will teach you the eight unruly horses and eight faults in horses, the eight unruly men and eight faults in men.
an8.14:1.2 | S: Listen and apply your mind well, I will speak.” | T: Listen and pay close attention. I will speak.”
an8.14:1.3 | S: “Yes, sir,” they replied. | T: “As you say, lord,” the monks responded to him.
an8.14:1.4 | S: The Buddha said this: | T: The Blessed One said:
an8.14:2.1 | S: “And what, mendicants, are the eight wild colts and eight defects in horses? | T: “Now, which are the eight unruly horses and eight faults in horses?
an8.14:2.2 | S: Firstly, when the trainer says ‘giddyup!’ and spurs and goads them on, some wild colts back right up and spin the chariot behind them. | T: “There is the case where some unruly horses—when goaded, ordered, and told ‘Go!‘ by the charioteer—back up and push the chariot back with their hindquarters.
an8.14:2.3 | S: Some wild colts are like that. | T: Some unruly horses are like this.
an8.14:2.4 | S: This is the first defect of a horse. | T: This is the first fault in a horse.
an8.14:3.1 | S: Furthermore, when the trainer says ‘giddyup!’ and spurs and goads them on, some wild colts jump back, wreck the hub, and break the triple rod. | T: “Then again, some unruly horses—when goaded, ordered, and told ‘Go!‘ by the charioteer—jump back and hit the carriage railing, breaking the triple bar.
an8.14:3.2 | S: Some wild colts are like that. | T: Some unruly horses are like this.
an8.14:3.3 | S: This is the second defect of a horse. | T: This is the second fault in a horse.
an8.14:4.1 | S: Furthermore, when the trainer says ‘giddyup!’ and spurs and goads them on, some wild colts shake the draft-pole off their thigh and trample it. | T: “Then again, some unruly horses—when goaded, ordered, and told ‘Go!‘ by the charioteer—kick the chariot pole and stomp on it.
an8.14:4.2 | S: Some wild colts are like that. | T: Some unruly horses are like this.
an8.14:4.3 | S: This is the third defect of a horse. | T: This is the third fault in a horse.
an8.14:5.1 | S: Furthermore, when the trainer says ‘giddyup!’ and spurs and goads them on, some wild colts take a wrong turn, sending the chariot off track. | T: “Then again, some unruly horses—when goaded, ordered, and told ‘Go!‘ by the charioteer—go off the road and make the chariot turn over.
an8.14:5.2 | S: Some wild colts are like that. | T: Some unruly horses are like this.
an8.14:5.3 | S: This is the fourth defect of a horse. | T: This is the fourth fault in a horse.
an8.14:6.1 | S: Furthermore, when the trainer says ‘giddyup!’ and spurs and goads them on, some wild colts rear up and strike out with their fore-legs. | T: “Then again, some unruly horses—when goaded, ordered, and told ‘Go!‘ by the charioteer—rear up and paw the air.
an8.14:6.2 | S: Some wild colts are like that. | T: Some unruly horses are like this.
an8.14:6.3 | S: This is the fifth defect of a horse. | T: This is the fifth fault in a horse.
an8.14:7.1 | S: Furthermore, when the trainer says ‘giddyup!’ and spurs and goads them on, some wild colts ignore the trainer and the goad, spit out the bit, and go wherever they want. | T: “Then again, some unruly horses—when goaded, ordered, and told ‘Go!‘ by the charioteer—not heeding the goad, bite through the bit with their teeth and go where they will.
an8.14:7.2 | S: Some wild colts are like that. | T: Some unruly horses are like this.
an8.14:7.3 | S: This is the sixth defect of a horse. | T: This is the sixth fault in a horse.
