## an8.19
an8.19:1.1 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying in Verañjā at the root of a neem tree dedicated to Naḷeru. | T: On one occasion the Blessed One was staying near Verañja at the root of Naḷeru’s neem tree.
an8.19:1.2 | S: Then Pahārāda, lord of titans, went up to the Buddha, bowed, and stood to one side. The Buddha said to him, | T: Then Pahārāda the asura-king went to the Blessed One and, on arrival, having bowed down to him, stood to one side. As he was standing there, the Blessed One said to him,
an8.19:2.1 | S: “Well, Pahārāda, do the titans love the ocean?” | T: “Do the asuras, Pahārāda, take great joy in the great ocean?”
an8.19:2.2 | S: “Sir, they do indeed.” | T: “The asuras take great joy in the great ocean, lord.”
an8.19:2.3 | S: “But seeing what incredible and amazing things do the titans love the ocean?” | T: “And how many, Pahārāda, are the amazing & astounding qualities of the great ocean because of which, as they see them again & again, the asuras take great joy in the ocean?”
an8.19:2.4 | S: “Sir, seeing eight incredible and amazing things the titans love the ocean. | T: “Lord, there are eight amazing & astounding qualities of the great ocean because of which, as they see them again & again, the asuras take great joy in the great ocean.
an8.19:2.5 | S: What eight? | T: Which eight?
an8.19:2.6 | S: The ocean gradually slants, slopes, and inclines, with no abrupt precipice. | T: “[1] Lord, the great ocean has a gradual shelf, a gradual slope, a gradual inclination, with a sudden drop-off only after a long stretch.
an8.19:2.7 | P: Yampi, bhante, mahāsamuddo anupubbaninno anupubbapoṇo anupubbapabbhāro, na āyatakeneva papāto; | T: The fact that the great ocean has a gradual shelf, a gradual slope, a gradual inclination, with a sudden drop-off only after a long stretch:
an8.19:2.8 | S: This is the first thing the titans love about the ocean. | T: This is the first amazing & astounding quality of the great ocean because of which, as they see it again & again, the asuras take great joy in the great ocean.
an8.19:3.1 | S: Furthermore, the ocean is consistent and doesn’t overflow its boundaries. | T: “[2] And furthermore, lord, the great ocean is stable and does not overstep its tideline.…
an8.19:3.2 | P: Yampi, bhante, mahāsamuddo ṭhitadhammo velaṁ nātivattati; | T: (none)
an8.19:3.3 | S: This is the second thing the titans love about the ocean. | T: This is the second amazing & astounding quality of the great ocean because of which, as they see it again & again, the asuras take great joy in the great ocean.
an8.19:4.1 | S: Furthermore, the ocean doesn’t accommodate a carcass, but quickly carries it to the shore and strands it on the beach. | T: “[3] And furthermore, lord, the great ocean does not tolerate a dead body. Any dead body in the great ocean gets quickly washed to the shore and thrown up on dry land.…
an8.19:4.2 | P: Yampi, bhante, mahāsamuddo na matena kuṇapena saṁvasati, yaṁ hoti mahāsamudde mataṁ kuṇapaṁ, taṁ khippameva tīraṁ vāheti, thalaṁ ussāreti; | T: (none)
an8.19:4.3 | S: This is the third thing the titans love about the ocean. | T: This is the third amazing & astounding quality of the great ocean because of which, as they see it again & again, the asuras take great joy in the great ocean.
an8.19:5.1 | S: Furthermore, when they reach the ocean, all the great rivers—that is, | T: “[4] And furthermore, lord, whatever great rivers there are—
an8.19:5.2 | S: the Ganges, Yamunā, Aciravatī, Sarabhū, and Mahī—lose their names and clans and are simply reckoned as ‘the ocean’. | T: such as the Ganges, the Yamunā, the Aciravatī, the Sarabhū, the Mahī—on reaching the great ocean, give up their former names and are classed simply as ‘great ocean’.…
an8.19:5.3 | P: Yampi, bhante, yā kāci mahānadiyo, seyyathidaṁ—gaṅgā yamunā aciravatī sarabhū mahī, tā mahāsamuddaṁ patvā jahanti purimāni nāmagottāni, ‘mahāsamuddo’ tveva saṅkhaṁ gacchanti; | T: (none)
an8.19:5.4 | S: This is the fourth thing the titans love about the ocean. | T: This is the fourth amazing & astounding quality of the great ocean because of which, as they see it again & again, the asuras take great joy in the great ocean.
an8.19:6.1 | S: Furthermore, for all the world’s streams that reach it, and the showers that fall from the sky, the ocean never empties or fills up. | T: “[5] And furthermore, lord, though the rivers of the world pour into the great ocean, and rains fall from the sky, no swelling or diminishing in the great ocean for that reason can be discerned.…
an8.19:6.2 | P: Yampi, bhante, yā ca loke savantiyo mahāsamuddaṁ appenti yā ca antalikkhā dhārā papatanti, na tena mahāsamuddassa ūnattaṁ vā pūrattaṁ vā paññāyati; | T: (none)
an8.19:6.3 | S: This is the fifth thing the titans love about the ocean. | T: This is the fifth amazing & astounding quality of the great ocean because of which, as they see it again & again, the asuras take great joy in the great ocean.
an8.19:7.1 | S: Furthermore, the ocean has just one taste, the taste of salt. | T: “[6] And furthermore, lord, the great ocean has a single taste: that of salt.…
an8.19:7.2 | P: Yampi, bhante, mahāsamuddo ekaraso loṇaraso; | T: (none)
an8.19:7.3 | S: This is the sixth thing the titans love about the ocean. | T: This is the sixth amazing & astounding quality of the great ocean because of which, as they see it again & again, the asuras take great joy in the great ocean.
an8.19:8.1 | S: Furthermore, the ocean is full of many kinds of treasures, such as pearls, gems, beryl, conch, quartz, coral, silver, native gold, rubies, and emeralds. | T: “[7] And furthermore, lord, the great ocean has these many treasures of various kinds: pearls, sapphires, lapis lazulis, shells, quartz, coral, silver, gold, rubies, & cat’s eyes.…
an8.19:8.2 | P: Yampi, bhante, mahāsamuddo bahuratano anekaratano; tatrimāni ratanāni, seyyathidaṁ—muttā maṇi veḷuriyo saṅkho silā pavāḷaṁ rajataṁ jātarūpaṁ lohitako masāragallaṁ; | T: (none)
an8.19:8.3 | S: This is the seventh thing the titans love about the ocean. | T: This is the seventh amazing & astounding quality of the great ocean because of which, as they see it again & again, the asuras take great joy in the great ocean.
an8.19:9.1 | S: Furthermore, many great beings live in the ocean, such as leviathans, leviathan-gulpers, leviathan-gulper-gulpers, titans, dragons, and centaurs. In the ocean there are life-forms a hundred leagues long, or even two hundred, three hundred, four hundred, or five hundred leagues long. | T: “[8] And furthermore, lord, the great ocean is the abode of such mighty beings as these: whales, whale-eaters, & whale-eater-eaters; asuras, nāgas, & gandhabbas. There are in the great ocean beings one hundred leagues long, two hundred… three hundred… four hundred… five hundred leagues long.
an8.19:9.2 | P: Yampi, bhante, mahāsamuddo mahataṁ bhūtānaṁ āvāso; tatrime bhūtā—timi timiṅgalo timirapiṅgalo asurā nāgā gandhabbā; santi mahāsamudde yojanasatikāpi attabhāvā …pe… tiyojana … catuyojana … pañcayojanasatikāpi attabhāvā; | T: The fact that the great ocean is the abode of such mighty beings as these: whales, whale-eaters, & whale-eater-eaters; asuras, nāgas, & gandhabbas; and there are in the great ocean beings one hundred leagues long, two hundred… three hundred… four hundred… five hundred leagues long:
an8.19:9.3 | S: This is the eighth thing the titans love about the ocean. | T: This is the eighth amazing & astounding quality of the great ocean because of which, as they see it again & again, the asuras take great joy in the great ocean.
an8.19:9.4 | S: Seeing these eight incredible and amazing things the titans love the ocean. | T: “These, lord, are the eight amazing & astounding qualities of the great ocean because of which, as they see them again & again, the asuras take great joy in the great ocean.
an8.19:10.1 | S: Well, sir, do the mendicants love this teaching and training?” | T: “But, lord, do the monks take great joy in this Dhamma & Vinaya?”
an8.19:10.2 | S: “They do indeed, Pahārāda.” | T: “The monks take great joy in this Dhamma & Vinaya, Pahārada.”
an8.19:10.3 | S: “But seeing how many incredible and amazing things do the mendicants love this teaching and training?” | T: “And how many, lord, are the amazing & astounding qualities of this Dhamma & Vinaya because of which, as they see them again & again, the monks take great joy in this Dhamma & Vinaya?”
an8.19:10.4 | S: “Seeing eight incredible and amazing things, Pahārāda, the mendicants love this teaching and training. | T: “Pahārāda, there are eight amazing & astounding qualities of this Dhamma & Vinaya because of which, as they see them again & again, the monks take great joy in this Dhamma & Vinaya.
an8.19:10.5 | S: What eight? | T: Which eight?
an8.19:10.6 | S: The ocean gradually slants, slopes, and inclines, with no abrupt precipice. | T: “[1] Just as the great ocean has a gradual shelf, a gradual slope, a gradual inclination, with a sudden drop-off only after a long stretch;
an8.19:10.7 | S: In the same way in this teaching and training the penetration to enlightenment comes from gradual training, progress, and practice, not abruptly. | T: in the same way, Pahārāda, this Dhamma & Vinaya has a gradual training, a gradual performance, a gradual practice, with a penetration to gnosis only after a long stretch.
an8.19:10.8 | P: Yampi, pahārāda, imasmiṁ dhammavinaye anupubbasikkhā anupubbakiriyā anupubbapaṭipadā, na āyatakeneva aññāpaṭivedho; | T: The fact that this Dhamma & Vinaya has a gradual training, a gradual performance, a gradual practice, with a penetration to gnosis only after a long stretch:
an8.19:10.9 | S: This is the first thing the mendicants love about this teaching and training. | T: This is the first amazing & astounding quality of this Dhamma & Vinaya because of which, as they see it again & again, the monks take great joy in this Dhamma & Vinaya.
an8.19:11.1 | S: The ocean is consistent and doesn’t overflow its boundaries. | T: “[2] And furthermore, just as the great ocean is stable and does not overstep its tideline;
an8.19:11.2 | S: In the same way, when a training rule is laid down for my disciples they wouldn’t break it even for the sake of their own life. | T: in the same way, Pahārāda, my disciples do not—even for the sake of their lives—overstep the training rules I have formulated for them.…
an8.19:11.3 | P: Yampi, pahārāda, mayā sāvakānaṁ sikkhāpadaṁ paññattaṁ taṁ mama sāvakā jīvitahetupi nātikkamanti; | T: (none)
an8.19:11.4 | S: This is the second thing the mendicants love about this teaching and training. | T: This is the second amazing & astounding quality of this Dhamma & Vinaya because of which, as they see it again & again, the monks take great joy in this Dhamma & Vinaya.
an8.19:12.1 | S: The ocean doesn’t accommodate a carcass, but quickly carries it to the shore and strands it on the beach. | T: “[3] And furthermore, just as the great ocean does not tolerate a dead body—any dead body in the great ocean getting quickly washed to the shore and thrown up on dry land—
an8.19:13.1 | S: In the same way, the Saṅgha doesn’t accommodate an individual who is unethical, of bad qualities, filthy, with suspicious behavior, underhand, no true ascetic or spiritual practitioner—though claiming to be one—rotten inside, festering, and depraved. But they quickly gather and expel them. Even if they are sitting in the middle of the Saṅgha, they’re far from the Saṅgha, and the Saṅgha is far from them. | T: in the same way, Pahārāda, if an individual is unprincipled, evil, unclean & suspect in his undertakings, hidden in his actions—not a contemplative though claiming to be one, not leading the holy life though claiming to do so, inwardly rotten, oozing with desire, filthy by nature—the community has no affiliation with him. Having quickly gathered together, they suspend him from the community. Even though he may be sitting in the midst of the community of monks, he is far from the community, and the community far from him.…
an8.19:13.2 | P: Yampi, pahārāda, yo so puggalo dussīlo pāpadhammo asucisaṅkassarasamācāro paṭicchannakammanto assamaṇo samaṇapaṭiñño abrahmacārī brahmacāripaṭiñño antopūti avassuto kasambujāto, na tena saṅgho saṁvasati; khippameva naṁ sannipatitvā ukkhipati; kiñcāpi so hoti majjhe bhikkhusaṅghassa sannisinno, atha kho so ārakāva saṅghamhā saṅgho ca tena; | T: (none)
an8.19:13.3 | S: This is the third thing the mendicants love about this teaching and training. | T: This is the third amazing & astounding quality of this Dhamma & Vinaya because of which, as they see it again & again, the monks take great joy in this Dhamma & Vinaya.
an8.19:14.1 | S: When they reach the ocean, all the great rivers—that is, the Ganges, Yamunā, Aciravatī, Sarabhū, and Mahī—lose their names and clans and are simply reckoned as ‘the ocean’. | T: “[4] And furthermore, just as whatever great rivers there are—such as the Ganges, the Yamunā, the Aciravatī, the Sarabhū, the Mahī—on reaching the great ocean, give up their former names and are classed simply as ‘great ocean’;
an8.19:14.2 | S: In the same way, when they go forth from the lay life to homelessness, all four classes—aristocrats, brahmins, peasants, and menials—lose their former names and clans and are simply reckoned as ‘ascetics who follow the Sakyan’. | T: in the same way, Pahārāda, when members of the four castes—noble warriors, brahmans, merchants, & workers—go forth from home to the homeless life in this Dhamma & Vinaya declared by the Tathāgata, they give up their former names & clans and are classed simply as ‘contemplatives, sons of the Sakyan’.…
an8.19:14.3 | P: Yampi, pahārāda, cattārome vaṇṇā—khattiyā, brāhmaṇā, vessā, suddā, te tathāgatappavedite dhammavinaye agārasmā anagāriyaṁ pabbajitvā jahanti purimāni nāmagottāni, ‘samaṇā sakyaputtiyā’ tveva saṅkhaṁ gacchanti; | T: (none)
an8.19:14.4 | S: This is the fourth thing the mendicants love about this teaching and training. | T: This is the fourth amazing & astounding quality of this Dhamma & Vinaya because of which, as they see it again & again, the monks take great joy in this Dhamma & Vinaya.
an8.19:15.1 | S: For all the world’s streams that reach it, and the showers that fall from the sky, the ocean never empties or fills up. | T: “[5] And furthermore, just as the rivers of the world pour into the great ocean, and rains fall from the sky, but no swelling or diminishing in the great ocean for that reason can be discerned;
an8.19:15.2 | S: In the same way, though several mendicants become fully extinguished in the element of extinguishment with no residue, the element of extinguishment never empties or fills up. | T: in the same way, Pahārāda, although many monks are totally unbound into the property of unbinding with no fuel remaining, no swelling or diminishing in the property of unbinding for that reason can be discerned.…
an8.19:15.3 | P: Yampi, pahārāda, bahū cepi bhikkhū anupādisesāya nibbānadhātuyā parinibbāyanti, na tena nibbānadhātuyā ūnattaṁ vā pūrattaṁ vā paññāyati; | T: (none)
an8.19:15.4 | S: This is the fifth thing the mendicants love about this teaching and training. | T: This is the fifth amazing & astounding quality of this Dhamma & Vinaya because of which, as they see it again & again, the monks take great joy in this Dhamma & Vinaya.
an8.19:16.1 | S: The ocean has just one taste, the taste of salt. | T: “[6] And furthermore, just as the great ocean has a single taste—that of salt—
an8.19:16.2 | S: In the same way, this teaching and training has one taste, the taste of freedom. | T: in the same way, Pahārāda, this Dhamma & Vinaya has a single taste: that of release.…
an8.19:16.3 | P: Yampi, pahārāda, ayaṁ dhammavinayo ekaraso, vimuttiraso; | T: (none)
an8.19:16.4 | S: This is the sixth thing the mendicants love about this teaching and training. | T: This is the sixth amazing & astounding quality of this Dhamma & Vinaya because of which, as they see it again & again, the monks take great joy in this Dhamma & Vinaya.
an8.19:17.1 | S: The ocean is full of many kinds of treasures, such as pearls, gems, beryl, conch, quartz, coral, silver, native gold, rubies, and emeralds. | T: “[7] And furthermore, just as the great ocean has these many treasures of various kinds—pearls, sapphires, lapis lazulis, shells, quartz, coral, silver, gold, rubies, & cat’s eyes—
an8.19:17.2 | S: In the same way, this teaching and training is full of many kinds of treasures, such as the four kinds of mindfulness meditation, the four right efforts, the four bases of psychic power, the five faculties, the five powers, the seven awakening factors, and the noble eightfold path. | T: in the same way, Pahārāda, this Dhamma & Vinaya has these many treasures of various kinds: the four establishings of mindfulness, the four right exertions, the four bases of power, the five faculties, the five strengths, the seven factors for awakening, the noble eightfold path .…
an8.19:17.3 | P: Yampi, pahārāda, ayaṁ dhammavinayo bahuratano anekaratano; tatrimāni ratanāni, seyyathidaṁ—cattāro satipaṭṭhānā, cattāro sammappadhānā, cattāro iddhipādā, pañcindriyāni, pañca balāni, satta bojjhaṅgā, ariyo aṭṭhaṅgiko maggo; | T: (none)
an8.19:17.4 | S: This is the seventh thing the mendicants love about this teaching and training. | T: This is the seventh amazing & astounding quality of this Dhamma & Vinaya because of which, as they see it again & again, the monks take great joy in this Dhamma & Vinaya.
an8.19:18.1 | S: Many great beings live in the ocean, such as leviathans, leviathan-gulpers, leviathan-gulper-gulpers, titans, dragons, and centaurs. In the ocean there are life-forms a hundred leagues long, or even two hundred, three hundred, four hundred, or five hundred leagues long. | T: “[8] And furthermore, just as the great ocean is the abode of such mighty beings as these: whales, whale-eaters, & whale-eater-eaters; asuras, nāgas, & gandhabbas, and there are in the great ocean beings one hundred leagues long, two hundred… three hundred… four hundred… five hundred leagues long;
an8.19:18.2 | S: In the same way, great beings live in this teaching and training, and these are those beings. The stream-enterer and the one practicing to realize the fruit of stream-entry. The once-returner and the one practicing to realize the fruit of once-return. The non-returner and the one practicing to realize the fruit of non-return. The perfected one, and the one practicing for perfection. | T: in the same way, Pahārāda, this Dhamma & Vinaya is the abode of such mighty beings as these: stream-winners & those practicing to realize the fruit of stream-entry; once-returners & those practicing to realize the fruit of once-returning; non-returners & those practicing to realize the fruit of non-returning; arahants & those practicing for arahantship.
an8.19:18.3 | P: Yampi, pahārāda, ayaṁ dhammavinayo mahataṁ bhūtānaṁ āvāso; tatrime bhūtā—sotāpanno sotāpattiphalasacchikiriyāya paṭipanno, sakadāgāmī sakadāgāmiphalasacchikiriyāya paṭipanno, anāgāmī anāgāmiphalasacchikiriyāya paṭipanno, arahā arahattāya paṭipanno; | T: The fact that this Dhamma & Vinaya is the abode of such mighty beings as these—stream-winners & those practicing to realize the fruit of stream-entry; once-returners & those practicing to realize the fruit of once-returning; non-returners & those practicing to realize the fruit of non-returning; arahants & those practicing for arahantship:
an8.19:18.4 | S: This is the eighth thing the mendicants love about this teaching and training. | T: This is the eighth amazing & astounding quality of this Dhamma & Vinaya because of which, as they see it again & again, the monks take great joy in this Dhamma & Vinaya.
an8.19:19.1 | S: Seeing these eight incredible and amazing things, Pahārāda, the mendicants love this teaching and training.” | T: “These, Pahārāda, are the eight amazing & astounding qualities of this Dhamma & Vinaya because of which, as they see them again & again, the monks take great joy in this Dhamma & Vinaya.”
an8.19:19.2 | P: Navamaṁ. | T: (none)

## an8.24
an8.24:1.1 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Āḷavī, at Āḷavī’s premier shrine. | T: On one occasion the Blessed One was staying near Āḷavī at the Aggāḷava Shrine.
an8.24:1.2 | S: Then the householder Hatthaka of Āḷavī, escorted by around five hundred lay followers, went up to the Buddha, bowed, and sat down to one side. The Buddha said to Hatthaka: | T: Then Hatthaka of Āḷavī, surrounded by approximately 500 (other) lay followers, went to the Blessed One. On arrival, having bowed down to the Blessed One, he sat to one side. As he was sitting there the Blessed One said to him,
an8.24:1.3 | S: “Hatthaka, you have a large congregation. | T: “Large is your following, Hatthaka.
an8.24:1.4 | S: How do you bring together such a large congregation?” | T: How have you won over this large following?”
an8.24:1.5 | S: “Sir, I bring together such a large congregation by using the four ways of being inclusive as taught by the Buddha. | T: “Lord, I have won over this large following through the four grounds for the bonds of fellowship taught by the Blessed One.
an8.24:1.6 | S: When I know that they | T: When I know that,
an8.24:1.7 | S: can be included by a gift, I include them by giving a gift. | T: ‘This person is to be won over by giving,’ then I win him/her over by giving.
an8.24:1.8 | S: When I know that they | T: When I know that,
an8.24:1.9 | S: can be included by kindly words, I include them by kindly words. | T: ‘This person is to be won over by kind words,’ then I win him/her over by kind words.
an8.24:1.10 | S: When I know that they | T: When I know that,
an8.24:1.11 | S: can be included by taking care of them, I include them by caring for them. | T: ‘This person is to be won over by beneficial help,’ then I win him/her over by beneficial help.
an8.24:1.12 | S: When I know that they | T: When I know that,
an8.24:1.13 | S: can be included by equality, I include them by treating them equally. | T: ‘This person is to be won over by consistency,’ then I win him/her over by consistency.
an8.24:1.14 | S: But also, worthy sir, my family is wealthy. | T: Awed by the wealth of my family,
an8.24:1.15 | S: They wouldn’t think that a poor person was worth listening to in the same way.” | T: they regard me as worth listening to, which would not be the case if I were poor.”
an8.24:1.16 | S: “Good, good, Hatthaka! | T: “It’s good, Hatthaka,
an8.24:1.17 | S: This is the right way to bring together a large congregation. | T: it’s very good that this is the means by which you have won over a large following.
an8.24:1.18 | S: Whether in the past, future, or present, all those who have brought together a large congregation have done so by using these four ways of being inclusive.” | T: All those in the past who have won over a large following have done so by means of these four same grounds for the bonds of fellowship.
an8.24:1.19 | P: Yepi hi keci, hatthaka, anāgatamaddhānaṁ mahatiṁ parisaṁ saṅgaṇhissanti, sabbe te imeheva catūhi saṅgahavatthūhi mahatiṁ parisaṁ saṅgaṇhissanti. | T: All those in the future who will win over a large following will do so by means of these four same grounds for the bonds of fellowship.
an8.24:1.20 | P: Yepi hi keci, hatthaka, etarahi mahatiṁ parisaṁ saṅgaṇhanti, sabbe te imeheva catūhi saṅgahavatthūhi mahatiṁ parisaṁ saṅgaṇhantī”ti. | T: All those at present who are winning over a large following do so by means of these four same grounds for the bonds of fellowship.”
an8.24:2.1 | S: Then the Buddha educated, encouraged, fired up, and inspired Hatthaka of Āḷavī with a Dhamma talk, after which he got up from his seat, bowed, and respectfully circled the Buddha, keeping him on his right, before leaving. | T: Then, having been instructed, urged, roused, & encouraged by the Blessed One with a talk on Dhamma, Hatthaka of Āḷavī got up from his seat, bowed down to the Blessed One, circled him—keeping him on his right—and left.
an8.24:2.2 | S: Then, not long after Hatthaka had left, the Buddha addressed the mendicants: | T: Not long after he had left, the Blessed One said to the monks,
an8.24:2.3 | S: “Mendicants, you should remember the householder Hatthaka of Āḷavī as someone who has eight amazing and incredible qualities. | T: “Monks, remember Hatthaka of Āḷavī as being endowed with eight amazing, astounding qualities.

