 succeeded in 0ms:
an8.24:2.4 | S: What eight? | T: Which eight?
an8.24:2.5 | S: He’s faithful, | T: Hatthaka of Āḷavī is endowed with conviction.
an8.24:2.6 | S: ethical, | T: He is virtuous.
an8.24:2.7 | S: conscientious, | T: He has a sense of shame.
an8.24:2.8 | S: prudent, | T: He has a sense of compunction (over the results of unskillful actions).
an8.24:2.9 | S: learned, | T: He is learned.
an8.24:2.10 | S: generous, | T: He is generous.
an8.24:2.11 | S: wise, | T: He is discerning.
an8.24:2.12 | S: and has few wishes. | T: He is modest.
an8.24:2.13 | S: You should remember the householder Hatthaka of Āḷavī as someone who possesses these eight amazing and incredible qualities.” | T: Remember Hatthaka of Āḷavī as being endowed with these eight amazing, astounding qualities.”
an8.24:2.14 | P: Catutthaṁ. | T: (none)

## an8.26
an8.26:1.1 | S: At one time the Buddha was staying near Rājagaha in Jīvaka’s Mango Grove. | T: I have heard that on one occasion the Blessed One was staying near Rājagaha in Jīvaka’s Mango Grove.
an8.26:1.2 | S: Then Jīvaka Komārabhacca went up to the Buddha, bowed, sat down to one side, and said to him, | T: Then Jīvaka Komārabhacca went to the Blessed One and, on arrival, having bowed down to the Blessed One, sat to one side. As he was sitting there he said to the Blessed One:
an8.26:1.3 | S: “Sir, how is a lay follower defined?” | T: “Lord, to what extent is one a lay follower?”
an8.26:1.4 | S: “Jīvaka, when you’ve gone for refuge to the Buddha, the teaching, and the Saṅgha, | T: “Jīvaka, when one has gone to the Buddha for refuge, has gone to the Dhamma for refuge, and has gone to the Saṅgha for refuge,
an8.26:1.5 | S: you’re considered to be a lay follower.” | T: then to that extent is one a lay follower.”
an8.26:2.1 | S: “But how is an ethical lay follower defined?” | T: “And to what extent, venerable sir, is one a virtuous lay follower?”
an8.26:2.2 | S: “When a lay follower doesn’t kill living creatures, steal, commit sexual misconduct, lie, or consume intoxicants of beer, wine, and liquor, | T: “Jīvaka, when a lay follower abstains from taking life, from stealing, from sexual misconduct, from lying, and from fermented & distilled drinks that lead to heedlessness,
an8.26:2.3 | S: they’re considered to be an ethical lay follower.” | T: then to that extent is one a virtuous lay follower.”
an8.26:3.1 | S: “But how do we define a lay follower who is practicing to benefit themselves, not others?” | T: “And to what extent, venerable sir, is one a lay follower who practices for his own benefit but not that of others?”
an8.26:3.2 | S: “A lay follower is accomplished in faith, but doesn’t encourage others to do the same. They’re accomplished in ethical conduct … they’re accomplished in generosity … they like to see the mendicants … they like to hear the true teaching … they memorize the teachings … they examine the meaning … | T: “Jīvaka, when a lay follower himself is consummate in conviction but does not encourage others in the consummation of conviction; when he himself is consummate in virtue but does not encourage others in the consummation of virtue; when he himself is consummate in generosity but does not encourage others in the consummation of generosity; when he himself desires to see the monks but does not encourage others to see the monks; when he himself wants to hear the true Dhamma but does not encourage others to hear the true Dhamma; when he himself habitually remembers the Dhamma he has heard but does not encourage others to remember the Dhamma; when he himself explores the meaning of the Dhamma he has heard but does not encourage others to explore the meaning;
an8.26:3.3 | S: Understanding the meaning and the teaching, they practice in line with the teaching, but they don’t encourage others to do the same. | T: when he himself, knowing both the Dhamma & its meaning, practices the Dhamma in line with the Dhamma, but does not encourage others to practice the Dhamma in line with the Dhamma—
an8.26:3.4 | S: That’s how we define a lay follower who is practicing to benefit themselves, not others.” | T: then to that extent he is a lay follower who practices for his own benefit but not for the benefit of others.”
an8.26:4.1 | S: “But how do we define a lay follower who is practicing to benefit both themselves and others?” | T: “And to what extent, venerable sir, is one a lay follower who practices both for his own benefit & the benefit of others?”
an8.26:4.2 | S: “A lay follower is accomplished in faith and encourages others to do the same. | T: “Jīvaka, when a lay follower himself is consummate in conviction and encourages others in the consummation of conviction;
an8.26:4.3 | S: They’re accomplished in ethical conduct and encourage others to do the same. | T: when he himself is consummate in virtue and encourages others in the consummation of virtue;
an8.26:4.4 | S: They’re accomplished in generosity and encourage others to do the same. | T: when he himself is consummate in generosity and encourages others in the consummation of generosity;
an8.26:4.5 | S: They like to see the mendicants and encourage others to do the same. | T: when he himself desires to see the monks and encourages others to see the monks;
an8.26:4.6 | S: They like to hear the true teaching and encourage others to do the same. | T: when he himself wants to hear the true Dhamma and encourages others to hear the true Dhamma;
an8.26:4.7 | S: They readily memorize the teachings they’ve heard and encourage others to do the same. | T: when he himself habitually remembers the Dhamma he has heard and encourages others to remember the Dhamma;
an8.26:4.8 | S: They examine the meaning of the teachings they’ve memorized and encourage others to do the same. | T: when he himself explores the meaning of the Dhamma he has heard and encourages others to explore the meaning;
an8.26:4.9 | S: Understanding the meaning and the teaching, they practice in line with the teaching and they encourage others to do the same. | T: when he himself, knowing both the Dhamma & its meaning, practices the Dhamma in line with the Dhamma and encourages others to practice the Dhamma in line with the Dhamma—
an8.26:4.10 | S: That’s how we define a lay follower who is practicing to benefit both themselves and others.” | T: then to that extent he is a lay follower who practices both for his own benefit and for the benefit of others.”
an8.26:4.11 | P: Chaṭṭhaṁ. | T: (none)

## an8.28
an8.28:1.1 | S: Then Venerable Sāriputta went up to the Buddha, bowed, and sat down to one side. The Buddha said to him: | T: Then Ven. Sāriputta went to the Blessed One and, on arrival, bowed down to him and sat to one side. As he was sitting there, the Blessed One said to him,
an8.28:1.2 | S: “Sāriputta, how many powers does a mendicant who has ended the defilements have that qualify them to claim: | T: “Sāriputta, how many are the strengths of a monk whose effluents are ended, endowed with which he affirms the ending of the effluents (thus):
an8.28:1.3 | S: ‘My defilements have ended’?” | T: ‘The effluents are ended in me’?”
an8.28:1.4 | S: “Sir, a mendicant who has ended the defilements has eight powers that qualify them to claim: | T: “Eight, lord, are the strengths of a monk whose effluents are ended, endowed with which he affirms the ending of the effluents (thus):
an8.28:1.5 | S: ‘My defilements have ended.’ | T: ‘The effluents are ended in me.’
an8.28:2.1 | S: What eight? | T: Which eight?
an8.28:2.2 | S: Firstly, a mendicant with defilements ended has clearly seen with right wisdom all conditions as truly impermanent. | T: “There is the case where the inconstancy of all fabrications as they have come to be is well seen with right discernment by a monk whose effluents are ended.
an8.28:2.3 | S: This is a power that a mendicant who has ended the defilements relies on to claim: | T: The fact that the inconstancy of all fabrications as they have come to be is well seen with right discernment by a monk whose effluents are ended is a strength of a monk whose effluents are ended, with reference to which he affirms the ending of the effluents (thus):
an8.28:2.4 | S: ‘My defilements have ended.’ | T: ‘The effluents are ended in me.’
an8.28:3.1 | S: Furthermore, a mendicant with defilements ended has clearly seen with right wisdom that sensual pleasures are truly like a pit of glowing coals. | T: “And further, sensual passions as they have come to be are well seen with right discernment as analogous to hot charcoals by a monk whose effluents are ended.
an8.28:3.2 | S: This is a power that a mendicant who has ended the defilements relies on to claim: | T: The fact that sensual passions as they have come to be are well seen with right discernment as analogous to hot charcoals by a monk whose effluents are ended is also a strength of a monk whose effluents are ended, with reference to which he affirms the ending of the effluents (thus):
an8.28:3.3 | S: ‘My defilements have ended.’ | T: ‘The effluents are ended in me.’
an8.28:4.1 | S: Furthermore, the mind of a mendicant with defilements ended slants, slopes, and inclines to seclusion. They’re withdrawn, loving renunciation, and have totally eliminated defiling influences. | T: “And further, the mind of a monk whose effluents are ended inclines toward seclusion, leans toward seclusion, tends toward seclusion, stays in seclusion, delights in renunciation, entirely rid of the qualities that act as a basis for the effluents.
an8.28:4.2 | S: This is a power that a mendicant who has ended the defilements relies on to claim: | T: The fact that the mind of a monk whose effluents are ended inclines toward seclusion, leans toward seclusion, tends toward seclusion, stays in seclusion, delights in renunciation, entirely rid of the qualities that act as a basis for the effluents is also is a strength of a monk whose effluents are ended, with reference to which he affirms the ending of the effluents (thus):
an8.28:4.3 | S: ‘My defilements have ended.’ | T: ‘The effluents are ended in me.’
an8.28:5.1 | S: Furthermore, a mendicant with defilements ended has well developed the four kinds of mindfulness meditation. | T: “And further, the four establishings of mindfulness are developed, well-developed by a monk whose effluents are ended.
an8.28:5.2 | S: This is a power that a mendicant who has ended the defilements relies on to claim: | T: The fact that the four establishings of mindfulness are developed, well-developed by a monk whose effluents are ended is also is a strength of a monk whose effluents are ended, with reference to which he affirms the ending of the effluents (thus):
an8.28:5.3 | S: ‘My defilements have ended.’ | T: ‘The effluents are ended in me.’
an8.28:6.1 | S: Furthermore, a mendicant with defilements ended has well developed the four bases of psychic power … | T: “And further, the four bases of power are developed, well-developed by a monk whose effluents are ended.…
