an8.14:11.5 | S: I say that this individual is comparable to the wild colts who, when the trainer says ‘giddyup!’ and spurs and goads them on, jump back, wreck the hub, and break the triple rod. | T: He, I tell you, is just like the unruly horse who—when goaded, ordered, and told ‘Go!‘ by the charioteer—jumps back and hits the carriage railing, breaking the triple bar.
an8.14:11.6 | P: tathūpamāhaṁ, bhikkhave, imaṁ puggalaṁ vadāmi. | T: (none)
an8.14:11.7 | S: Some wild people are like that. | T: Some unruly men are like this.
an8.14:11.8 | S: This is the second defect of a person. | T: This is the second fault in a man.
an8.14:12.1 | S: Furthermore, the mendicants accuse a mendicant of an offense. | T: “Then again, the monks accuse a monk of an offense.

